const e=[{id:"sv-5-281",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A fixed sample of **150 activation orders** is checked every day and each order is classed as defective or not. The historical proportion defective is $\\bar{p}=0.06$. Today **18** orders were defective. What is the **UCL** of the np chart?",options:["11.91","14.82","17.73","18.00"],answer:2,hint:"np chart: $$UCL=n\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Constant subgroup $n=150$, $\\bar{p}=0.06$, so the centre line is $n\\bar{p}$."},{title:"Centre line",body:"$$n\\bar{p}=150\\times 0.06=9$$"},{title:"Substitute into the limit",body:"$$UCL=n\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}=9+3\\sqrt{9\\times 0.94}=9+3\\times 2.909=17.73$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Today's count of 18 is above 17.73, so Test 1 fails and the response plan is triggered."}],final:"UCL = 17.73 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Added only one sigma (9 + 2.91) instead of three.","Used 2 sigma (9 + 5.82) — control limits are set at 3 sigma.","","Used the c-chart sigma $\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}}$ and dropped the $(1-\\bar{p})$ term: 9 + 3 × 3 = 18."],trap:"np sigma is binomial: keep the (1 − p̄) factor — √(np̄) alone is the c chart.",bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-282",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A back-office team checks **100 customer contracts every day** and records **how many errors** it finds on them. One contract can contain several errors (wrong address, missing signature, wrong tariff code). A supervisor proposes an **np chart** because the sample size is constant. Which chart is MOST appropriate?",options:["np chart (number of defective contracts)","p chart (proportion of defective contracts)","I-MR chart of the daily error count","u chart (errors per contract)"],answer:3,hint:"Count of defects (a unit can have many) → c/u chart; count of defectives (unit is good or bad) → p/np chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"np and p charts track **defectives**: each unit is simply pass or fail. c and u charts track **defects**, where one unit can carry several flaws."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The team counts errors, and a contract can have more than one. That is defect data, so a defects chart is needed; the u chart plots defects per unit and the book notes it can do everything a c chart can."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A constant sample size does not make np correct — np needs pass/fail units. Converting error counts to an I-MR chart throws away the Poisson nature of the data."}],final:"Defects, not defectives → u chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["np counts defective units; here each contract can carry several errors, so the data are defects.","p is also a defectives chart (proportion of bad units), not a defects chart.","Attribute counts with a known area of opportunity belong on an attribute chart, not on an individuals chart.",""],trap:'"Constant sample size" is the np clue only when the data are defectives.',bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 531"},{id:"sv-5-283",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
NP Chart of Failed SIM Activations
Subgroup size n = 80
NP-bar = 2.000   UCL = 6.189   LCL = 0

Test Results for NP Chart of Failed SIM Activations
 * No points failed any test.
\`\`\`
Why does Minitab show **LCL = 0**?`,options:["Minitab always sets the LCL of attribute charts to zero","The process has no defectives in some subgroups, so the LCL is moved to the smallest observed count","An LCL of zero means the process is capable of zero defectives","The formula gives a negative value, and a count of defectives cannot be below zero, so the limit is set to 0"],answer:3,hint:"$LCL=n\\bar{p}-3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$; a negative result is replaced by 0.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"$$LCL=n\\bar{p}-3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}=2-3\\sqrt{2\\times 0.975}=2-4.19=-2.19$$"},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"A count of defective activations cannot be negative, so the limit is truncated to 0. With a small $n\\bar{p}$ the chart has no working lower limit, so Test 1 cannot flag an improvement below the centre line."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Attribute LCLs are not always zero — with a larger $n\\bar{p}$ they are positive. Control limits are the voice of the process, not a capability statement."}],final:"Negative computed LCL is set to 0 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Not always: with a large enough n·p̄ the attribute LCL is positive (e.g. n·p̄ = 15 gives a positive LCL).","Limits come from n and p̄, not from the smallest observed count.","Control limits describe the voice of the process; they say nothing about capability or a zero-defect process.",""],trap:"LCL = 0 is a truncation, not a target.",bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-284",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
NP Chart of Rejected Port-out Requests
Subgroup size n = 400
NP-bar = 12.00   UCL = 22.24   LCL = 1.76

Test Results for NP Chart of Rejected Port-out Requests
 * No points failed any test.
\`\`\`
The process owner asks what **percentage** of requests the stable process rejects on average. What is the answer?`,options:["0.44%","3.00%","5.56%","12.00%"],answer:1,hint:"Centre line of an np chart is $n\\bar{p}$, so $\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{n\\bar{p}}{n}$.",steps:[{title:"Read the right number",body:"The average proportion defective comes from the **centre line** (NP-bar), not from a control limit."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{n\\bar{p}}{n}=\\dfrac{12}{400}=0.03=3.00\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The book notes that the centre line of a p-type chart is also the process capability for attribute data: about 3% of requests are rejected."}],final:"p̄ = 3.00% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided the LCL (1.76) by n instead of the centre line.","","Divided the UCL (22.24) by n instead of the centre line.","Read NP-bar (a count of 12 per 400) as if it were already a percentage."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 540"},{id:"sv-5-285",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:"```\nNP Chart of Late Field Visits\nSubgroup size n = 250 (visits audited per week)\nNP-bar = 14.0\nPoint 9:  NP = 21\n```\nWhat does the value **21** plotted at point 9 represent?",options:["21 late visits counted in that week's sample of 250 visits","21% of that week's visits were late","21 lateness defects per visit, on average","The 21st late visit of the year"],answer:0,hint:"np chart y-axis = number of defective units in each (constant-size) subgroup.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An np chart plots the **number** of defective units in each subgroup of constant size $n$."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Point 9 is one week's subgroup of 250 audited visits; 21 of them were late (defective)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A proportion is what the p chart plots; defects per unit is the u chart; the chart is not a running total."}],final:"Count of defective units in the subgroup → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A percentage is the p chart statistic; 21 of 250 is 8.4%, not 21%.","Defects per unit is the u chart statistic, and a visit is either late or not.","Each point is a separate subgroup count, not a cumulative total (that would be a CuSum-type plot)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-286",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are true of an **np chart** EXCEPT:",options:["It plots the number of defective units in each subgroup","It is normally used when the subgroup size is constant","Its sigma is based on the binomial term $n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})$","Its control limits move up and down from subgroup to subgroup as the sample size changes"],answer:3,hint:"np = count of defectives, constant n, binomial sigma, flat limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: np tracks the number of defective units per subgroup, sample size usually constant and usually > 50; sigma $=\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$."},{title:"Find the false statement",body:"Limits that step up and down with the sample size are the signature of the **p chart** (and u chart) with unequal subgroups. With constant $n$, np limits are flat lines."}],final:"Varying limits belong to p/u charts → answer **D**",whyWrong:["True — np plots the count of defective units.","True — constant subgroup size is the np condition.","True — the np chart uses binomial sigma.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 540"},{id:"sv-5-287",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:3,stem:"A team checks a **constant 200 invoices** a day. If it plots the same data on a p chart and on an np chart (3-sigma limits, Test 1 only), both charts will flag **exactly the same days** as out of control. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"With constant n, every np value and np limit equals n times the p value and p limit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"np chart: plotted value $np_i$, limits $n\\bar{p}\\pm 3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$. p chart: plotted value $p_i$, limits $\\bar{p}\\pm 3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n}}$."},{title:"Apply",body:"Multiplying every p-chart quantity by the constant $n=200$ gives exactly the np chart: same picture, different scale. A day beyond the p limit is beyond the np limit too."},{title:"Conclude",body:"With a constant sample size the two charts are equivalent; the choice is about which unit (count or proportion) people find easier to read."}],final:"Same signals with constant n → **True** (answer **True**)",whyWrong:["","The np chart is just the p chart multiplied by a constant n, so a point cannot be inside one and outside the other."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539–540"},{id:"sv-5-288",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A field-service manager asks the Green Belt to add the **SLA line "no more than 8 failed repairs per day"** to the np chart of failed repairs (constant sample of 120 repairs a day). What is the BEST response?',options:["Keep specification and SLA lines off the control chart; judge the SLA separately with a capability or performance report","Add the SLA line and use it in place of the UCL so the chart reflects the customer","Add the SLA line beside the control limits so operators react to whichever line is crossed first","Replace the np chart with a p chart, because only p charts can carry an SLA line"],answer:0,hint:"Control limits = voice of the process; specifications = voice of the customer. The book advises never to put specifications on a control chart (pre-control is the only exception).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "never have specification limits on a Control Chart because of the confusion often generated." Control limits come from process data; specifications come from the customer.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The SLA of 8 failures is a customer requirement. Mixing it into the chart would make people react to an SLA breach as if it were a special cause, or ignore real special causes inside the SLA. The SLA is assessed through capability (e.g. % days over 8)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Replacing the UCL with the SLA destroys the statistical meaning of the chart; switching to a p chart does not change the rule."}],final:"Keep the SLA off the control chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The UCL is calculated from process variation; replacing it with an SLA means the chart no longer detects special causes.","Two kinds of line on one chart is exactly the confusion the book warns about.","No control chart type is meant to carry specification lines; only pre-control uses specification-based zones."],bookRef:"Book pp. 524–525, 543"},{id:"sv-5-289",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team monitors **average call-setup time** with subgroups of **n = 4** consecutive calls taken every 15 minutes. The data contain occasional very slow calls caused by known network retries, and the team is concerned that these extreme values are distorting its measure of within-subgroup spread. According to the book's chart-selection table, which chart addresses this concern?",options:["Xbar-R chart","I-MR chart","np chart","Xbar-S chart"],answer:3,hint:'Book: "Outliers can cause issues with Range (R) charts so Standard Deviation charts (S) used instead if concerned."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The range uses only the largest and smallest value in each subgroup, so one extreme value sets the whole range. The book recommends the S chart when outliers are a concern."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The data are continuous and subgrouped (n = 4), and the stated concern is outliers distorting the spread estimate, so an Xbar-S chart fits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Xbar-R is the usual choice for small subgroups but is the chart the book says outliers upset; I-MR is for n = 1; np is for attribute (defective) data."}],final:"Outlier concern with subgroups → Xbar-S → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Xbar-R is the default for n of 2–5 but the range is exactly the statistic that a single outlier distorts.","I-MR is for subgroups of one; the team already collects subgroups of four.","Call-setup time is continuous data; np is for counts of defective units.",""],trap:"Small subgroup size usually points to Xbar-R — but the book names outlier concern as a reason to switch to S.",bookRef:"Book p. 531"},{id:"sv-5-290",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why is the **standard deviation (S)** a better measure of within-subgroup spread than the **range (R)** when subgroups are large (for example n = 15)?",options:["s uses every value in the subgroup, whereas the range uses only the largest and smallest values","s is always smaller than R, so the S chart has narrower limits","The range cannot be calculated when n is larger than 10","s is not affected by special causes, so the S chart never gives false alarms"],answer:0,hint:"R = max − min (2 values); s uses all n values.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The range is built from only two observations. As n grows, more information is wasted, so R becomes a less efficient estimate of spread."},{title:"Apply",body:"The standard deviation uses all 15 values, so it estimates within-subgroup variation more precisely. That is why the selection chart sends subgroups of about 10+ to Xbar-S."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Comparing s and R directly is meaningless (they are on different scales and corrected with different constants); R can be calculated for any n; no chart is immune to special causes."}],final:"s uses all the data → answer **A**",whyWrong:["",'s and R are different statistics with different constants; "narrower limits" is not the reason.',"A range can be calculated for any subgroup size; it just becomes inefficient.","Special causes affect s too — detecting them is the point of the S chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-291",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Xbar-S Chart of Order Validation Time (s)     n = 12

Test Results for Xbar Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  17

Test Results for S Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  14, 17
\`\`\`
What should the team do FIRST?`,options:["Investigate the Xbar signal at point 17 first, because a shift in the mean matters more to customers","Ignore point 14, because only point 17 fails on both charts","Investigate the S chart signals first, because the Xbar limits are only valid when the variation chart is in control","Recalculate the limits without points 14 and 17 and re-run the chart"],answer:2,hint:"Read the variation chart (R, MR or S) first; the mean chart's limits are built from it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the R chart must be in control before the averages chart can be interpreted, because the averages limits use the within-subgroup variation. The same logic holds for the S chart (limits use $\\bar{s}$)."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"The S chart fails Test 1 at points 14 and 17. Unstable variation can make the Xbar limits wrong and the Xbar signal at 17 may be caused by that subgroup's unusual spread. Start the investigation on the S chart."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring point 14 discards a real signal; deleting points before finding a cause hides special causes."}],final:"Variation chart first → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Xbar chart cannot be trusted until the S chart is stable — the order matters.","A single Test 1 failure on the S chart is a special cause on its own; it does not need a matching Xbar signal.","","Points are removed from the limit calculation only after a special cause is found and removed, not before investigating."],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 539"},{id:"sv-5-292",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:"```\nXbar-S Chart of Ticket Triage Time (min)    n = 5\nXbar Chart:  UCL = 18.42   CL = 15.00   LCL = 11.58\nS Chart:     UCL = 5.01    CL (S-bar) = 2.40   LCL = 0\n```\nWhy is the **LCL of the S chart** equal to 0?",options:["Minitab sets the S-chart LCL to zero whenever s̄ is small","The process has been improved, so the lower limit was removed","Standard deviations are always zero at the lower limit because spread cannot be negative","For subgroups of 5 or fewer the constant B₃ is 0, so B₃ × s̄ = 0"],answer:3,hint:"$LCL_S=B_3\\bar{s}$, $UCL_S=B_4\\bar{s}$; B₃ = 0 for n ≤ 5.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The S chart limits are $LCL_S=B_3\\bar{s}$ and $UCL_S=B_4\\bar{s}$. The constant $B_3$ is 0 for subgroup sizes up to 5."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"With $n=5$: $LCL_S=0\\times 2.40=0$. It does not depend on the size of $\\bar{s}$."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'A positive B₃ (n ≥ 6) gives a positive LCL, so "always zero" is wrong; process improvement does not delete limits.'}],final:"B₃ = 0 for n = 5 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The LCL depends on B₃ (the subgroup size), not on how small s̄ is.","Limits are recalculated after a documented change; they are not removed.","For n ≥ 6, B₃ > 0 and the S-chart LCL is positive, so zero is not automatic.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-293",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following statements about the **Xbar-S chart** are correct EXCEPT:",options:["It is suited to larger subgroups, typically about 10 or more","The Xbar chart limits are calculated with the constant A₃ and $\\bar{s}$","It is the chart to use when each subgroup contains only one measurement","The S chart shows how the spread within subgroups changes over time"],answer:2,hint:"n = 1 → I-MR; n ≈ 2–9 → Xbar-R; n ≈ 10+ → Xbar-S.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's selection tree: continuous data, subgroup size 1 → I-MR; 2–5 → Xbar-R; 10+ → Xbar-S."},{title:"Find the false statement",body:"A standard deviation cannot be calculated from a single value, so subgroups of one need the I-MR chart, not Xbar-S."}],final:"n = 1 is I-MR territory → answer **C**",whyWrong:["True — the selection chart sends subgroups of 10+ to Xbar-S.","True — $UCL=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_3\\bar{s}$.","","True — the S chart tracks within-subgroup variation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530, 539"},{id:"sv-5-294",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the steps for building an **Xbar-S chart** from historical subgroups in order:

- **P** — Calculate the grand mean $\\bar{\\bar{x}}$ and the average standard deviation $\\bar{s}$
- **Q** — Calculate the mean and standard deviation of each subgroup
- **R** — Plot the subgroup means and standard deviations and apply the special-cause tests
- **S** — Calculate the limits with A₃, B₃ and B₄`,options:["Q → P → S → R","P → Q → S → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:0,hint:"Subgroup statistics → overall averages → limits → plot and test.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each limit formula uses the overall averages ($\\bar{\\bar{x}}$, $\\bar{s}$), and those averages are built from the individual subgroup statistics."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (each subgroup's $\\bar{x}_i$ and $s_i$) → P (average them) → S ($\\bar{\\bar{x}}\\pm A_3\\bar{s}$, $B_3\\bar{s}$, $B_4\\bar{s}$) → R (plot and test)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"You cannot average subgroup statistics you have not yet calculated, and the tests need limits before points can be judged."}],final:"Q → P → S → R → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The grand mean and s̄ are averages of the subgroup statistics, so those must be calculated first.","The limits need s̄ and the grand mean, which come from step P.","Points cannot be tested against limits that have not been calculated."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-295",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"On an Xbar-S chart, the limits of the **Xbar** chart are calculated from the average subgroup standard deviation $\\bar{s}$ (with the constant A₃), not from the average range. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"$UCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_3\\bar{s}$, $LCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}-A_3\\bar{s}$.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's Xbar-S formulas: $UCL=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_3\\bar{s}$ and $LCL=\\bar{\\bar{x}}-A_3\\bar{s}$."},{title:"Conclude",body:"The average range and A₂ belong to the Xbar-R chart. On an Xbar-S chart both halves use $\\bar{s}$."}],final:"A₃ with s̄ → **True** (answer **True**)",whyWrong:["","A₂ × R̄ is the Xbar-R formula; the Xbar-S chart uses A₃ × s̄."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-296",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Green Belt builds an Xbar-S chart of **ticket resolution time** with **one subgroup per day of 20 tickets**, drawn from the morning, evening and night shifts. The night shift is known to be much slower. Almost every daily mean sits very close to the centre line, and the chart has never signalled. What is the MOST likely problem?",options:["The subgroup size of 20 is too large for an S chart","The process is very stable, so the chart is working as intended","An I-MR chart must be used whenever data come from more than one shift","The subgroups are not rational: shift-to-shift differences are inside each subgroup, inflating $\\bar{s}$ and widening the Xbar limits"],answer:3,hint:"Rational subgroup = items made under very similar conditions in a short time; extra sources of variation inside subgroups hide special causes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: control limits depend on what sources of variation you put inside the subgroups. If other sources (operators, shifts, suppliers) are included, they become part of the "natural" variation and can no longer be detected.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Each daily subgroup mixes three shifts, so the slow night shift inflates every $s_i$. A large $\\bar{s}$ gives wide Xbar limits, so the means hug the centre line and nothing signals."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"n = 20 is well suited to Xbar-S. A quiet chart is not proof of stability when subgrouping is wrong. Separate charts or shift-based subgroups are the fix, not a rule that forces I-MR."}],final:"Irrational subgroups bury the shift effect → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Large subgroups (10+) are exactly where the S chart is recommended.","Means hugging the centre line here are a symptom of inflated within-subgroup spread, not of a stable process.","Multiple shifts call for rational subgrouping (or one chart per stream), not automatically for I-MR.",""],trap:'"Never signals" can mean the subgroups hide the special cause.',bookRef:"Book pp. 517, 528"},{id:"sv-5-297",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An upper tabular CuSum monitors **daily average ticket age** (hours) with target **T = 200** and reference value **K = 5**. The previous upper sum was $C^+_{i-1}=12$ and today's value is $x_i=210$. What is today's $C^+_i$?",options:["5","17","22","27"],answer:1,hint:"$$C^+_i=\\max\\left[0,\\;C^+_{i-1}+x_i-(T+K)\\right]$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$T=200$, $C^+_{i-1}=12$, $x_i=210$, and the reference value $K=k\\times\\sigma=5$ (already in data units)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$C^+_i=\\max\\left[0,\\;C^+_{i-1}+x_i-(T+K)\\right]$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$C^+_i=\\max\\left[0,\\;12+210-205\\right]=\\max\\left[0,\\;17\\right]=17$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The sum keeps growing while values stay above $T+K$; a signal is given only when it passes the decision interval H."}],final:"C⁺ = 17 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Dropped the previous sum: 210 − 205 = 5. A CuSum accumulates, so the earlier 12 must be carried forward.","","Forgot the reference value K: 12 + 210 − 200 = 22.","Added K instead of subtracting it: 12 + 210 − 195 = 27."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 542"},{id:"sv-5-298",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
CUSUM Chart of API Latency (ms)
Target = 500   sigma = 5   k = 0.5   h = 4    (K = 2.5, H = 20)
Upper CUSUM: C+(i) = max[0, C+(i-1) + x(i) - (Target + K)]

Point    x      C+
  1     506     3.5
  2     509    10.0
  3     504    11.5
  4     510    19.0
  5     508    24.5
  6     507    29.0
\`\`\`
An I chart of the same data (limits 485–515) shows no failed tests. At which point does the CuSum FIRST signal?`,options:["Point 2","Point 4","Point 5","No signal, because every value is inside the I-chart limits"],answer:2,hint:"CuSum signals when the cumulative sum exceeds the decision interval H.",steps:[{title:"Read the right number",body:"The decision interval is H = 20. Compare each C+ with 20, not each x with the I-chart limits."},{title:"Apply",body:"Point 4: 19.0 < 20 (no signal). Point 5: 24.5 > 20 → first signal."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Every value is only a little above target, so the I chart stays quiet; the CuSum adds these small deviations up and catches the sustained shift — exactly what it is for."}],final:"C⁺ first exceeds H at point 5 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Point 2 has the biggest single jump in x, but C+ = 10.0 is far below H = 20.","C+ = 19.0 is close to H but does not exceed it.","","The CuSum is designed to catch small sustained shifts that stay inside Shewhart limits."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 542"},{id:"sv-5-299",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A CuSum plot of **cumulative deviation from target** (target = 100 s) for a provisioning step shows:

\`\`\`
Point     x       Cumulative sum of (x - 100)
  9     100.5         0.3
 10      99.5        -0.2
 11     102.1         1.9
 12     101.8         3.7
 13     102.3         6.0
 14     101.9         7.9
 15     102.0         9.9
\`\`\`
From point 11 the line rises in a straight line, about **2 units per point**. What does this indicate?`,options:["The process mean shifted at about point 11 to roughly 102 s and has stayed there","The process mean is now about 109.9 s","The process mean is rising a little further with every new point (a trend)","The variation increased from point 11, but the mean is still on target"],answer:0,hint:"On a CuSum, a constant slope = a constant shift of the mean; slope ≈ (mean − target).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CuSum adds up deviations from target. If the mean is on target the line wanders around a flat level; if the mean sits above target by $\\delta$, the line climbs by about $\\delta$ per point."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"$$\\text{slope}=\\dfrac{9.9-(-0.2)}{15-10}=\\dfrac{10.1}{5}\\approx 2$$ So from point 11 the mean is about $100+2=102$ s. The change in slope marks where the shift began."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The cumulative value (9.9) is a running total, not the mean. A straight line means a constant offset; an ever-steeper curve would mean a drifting mean."}],final:"Constant slope ≈ 2 → mean shifted to ~102 s → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Read the running total (9.9) as if it were the current deviation of the mean.","A trend in the mean would make the CuSum curve bend more and more steeply; a straight line means a constant shift.","The individual values (101.8–102.3) are tight; it is their level, not their spread, that changed."],trap:"Straight rising line on a CuSum = step shift, not a trend.",bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 542"},{id:"sv-5-300",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following describe the **CuSum chart** EXCEPT:",options:["It plots the cumulative sum of deviations from a target","It is used when past data are as important as present data","It is the best chart for catching a single large, sudden spike in one observation","Its calculations are normally left to Minitab or other software"],answer:2,hint:"CuSum = cumulative deviations, small sustained shifts, past as important as present, computer-calculated.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: CuSum is used for the same reasons as EWMA (small shifts), except the past data are as important as present data; the calculations are complex and done with software."},{title:"Find the false statement",body:"A single big spike is caught fastest by a Shewhart chart with Test 1. A CuSum spreads the effect of one value across the running total and is built to detect small, sustained shifts."}],final:"Large single spike → Shewhart, not CuSum → answer **C**",whyWrong:["True — that is what is plotted.","True — the book's distinguishing feature versus EWMA.","","True — the book does not show the maths and says it needs automation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 542"},{id:"sv-5-301",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A CuSum chart plots each individual measurement against ±3σ limits around the process mean, in the same way as an I chart. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"CuSum plots accumulated deviations from target, compared with a decision interval.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CuSum plots the **cumulative sum** of deviations from a target, not the raw measurements."},{title:"Conclude",body:"Its decision rule is the decision interval (H) or a V-mask, not ±3σ limits around individual points. Plotting each value against ±3σ is the I chart."}],final:"Statement describes an I chart → **False** (answer **False**)",whyWrong:["The statement describes an individuals chart; a CuSum plots accumulated deviations from target.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 542"},{id:"sv-5-302",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A CuSum chart of **SMS delivery delay** signals at point 23. The team finds a mis-set retry timer, corrects it and records the action. What should happen to the CuSum next?",options:["Keep accumulating from the current value; the sum will drift back down by itself","Reset the cumulative sum to zero after the correction and continue monitoring from there","Change the target to the current average so the chart stops signalling","Stop using the CuSum and switch to an I chart, because the CuSum has done its job"],answer:1,hint:"After a confirmed special cause is corrected, restart the cumulative statistic so it reflects only the new state.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CuSum carries all past deviations forward. Once the cause is fixed, the old accumulated deviation no longer describes the process."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Restart the sum at zero after the correction (and record it). The chart then shows quickly whether the fix has brought the mean back to target."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Keeping the old total causes a long run of signals from a problem that is already fixed. Moving the target hides the shift. Switching charts throws away the small-shift sensitivity the team chose CuSum for."}],final:"Reset after correction → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The inflated total would keep signalling for many points after the fix and hide whether the fix worked.","","Moving the target to the shifted mean accepts the special cause instead of removing it.","The reason for using a CuSum (small sustained shifts) has not gone away."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 544, 548"},{id:"sv-5-303",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An EWMA chart uses $\\lambda=0.2$. The previous EWMA value was $z_{t-1}=50$ and the new observation is $x_t=60$. What is $z_t$?",options:["52","55","58","60"],answer:0,hint:"$$z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\lambda=0.2$, $x_t=60$, $z_{t-1}=50$."},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}=0.2\\times 60+0.8\\times 50=12+40=52$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The plotted point moves only 2 units toward the new value: the EWMA smooths noise and reacts gradually."}],final:"z = 52 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Took a simple average of 60 and 50 — that ignores the weighting factor λ.","Swapped the weights: 0.8 × 60 + 0.2 × 50 = 58.","Plotted the raw observation; that is only what an EWMA with λ = 1 would do."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-304",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
EWMA Chart of Daily Avg Handle Time (min)
Lambda = 0.2   Target = 50   Sigma = 2
(I chart of the same data: UCL = 56, LCL = 44)

Point     x       EWMA      UCL
  6     51.9     51.20     51.93
  7     53.1     51.58     51.96
  8     52.6     51.79     51.97
  9     53.0     52.03     51.98
 10     52.2     52.06     51.99
 11     53.4     52.33     51.99
\`\`\`
At which point does the EWMA chart FIRST signal?`,options:["Point 7","Point 9","Point 11","No signal, because no x value is beyond the I-chart UCL of 56"],answer:1,hint:"Compare the plotted EWMA value (not the raw x) with that point's EWMA limit.",steps:[{title:"Read the right column",body:"The EWMA chart plots the weighted values, not the raw measurements. Compare the EWMA column with the UCL column point by point."},{title:"Apply",body:"Point 8: 51.79 < 51.97. Point 9: 52.03 > 51.98 → first signal."},{title:"Interpret",body:"The raw values are all well inside the I-chart limits, but they sit consistently about 1.5σ above target. The EWMA accumulates that evidence and flags a small sustained shift."}],final:"EWMA first exceeds its UCL at point 9 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Point 7 has a high raw value (53.1), but the plotted EWMA (51.58) is below its UCL.","","Point 11 has the largest EWMA, but the chart had already signalled at point 9.","EWMA limits are much tighter than I-chart limits; comparing raw x with 56 is the wrong test."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 541"},{id:"sv-5-305",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A network probe reports **average latency every hour**. Consecutive hourly values are strongly **autocorrelated** (each hour looks like the previous one), and the team must detect **small shifts** in this output. According to the book's selection table, which chart is MOST appropriate?",options:["Xbar-R chart","Pre-control chart","p chart","EWMA chart"],answer:3,hint:'Book: EWMA — "small shift needs to be detected often because of autocorrelation of the output results"; used for individuals or averages of outputs.',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's variable-chart table lists EWMA for when a small shift needs to be detected, often because the output results are autocorrelated. It is used for individual values or averages of outputs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Hourly latency is a continuous output, one value per hour, autocorrelated, and the goal is small-shift detection, so EWMA fits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Xbar-R needs rational subgroups and is slow to catch small shifts. Pre-control is for set-up-critical outputs with spec-based zones. p is for proportion defective."}],final:"Small shifts in an autocorrelated output → EWMA → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Xbar-R detects large shifts well but is insensitive to small ones, and there are no rational subgroups here.","Pre-control uses specification-based zones and is meant for set-up qualification, not small-shift detection.","Latency is continuous; a p chart is for the proportion of defective units.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 531"},{id:"sv-5-306",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"For an EWMA chart of **individual observations**, how does the book say the σ in the control-limit formula should be estimated?",options:["The overall standard deviation of all data, including periods with special causes","$\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}$ from the historical data","One sixth of the specification width","The weighting factor λ multiplied by the target"],answer:1,hint:"EWMA limits: $\\bar{X}\\pm 3\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2-\\lambda}\\left[1-(1-\\lambda)^{2t}\\right]}$, σ from historical data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines σ as the standard deviation of historical data: pooled SD for subgroups, or $\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}$ for individual observations."},{title:"Apply",body:"Individual observations → short-term sigma from the average moving range, the same estimate an I-MR chart uses."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An overall SD including special-cause periods inflates sigma and widens limits. Specifications never set control limits. λ is a weight, not a measure of spread."}],final:"σ = MR̄ ÷ d₂ for individuals → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Including special-cause periods inflates sigma, so the chart becomes blind to the shifts it is meant to catch.","","Control limits come from process variation, never from specifications.","λ only sets how fast old data are forgotten; it is not a spread estimate."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-307",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does each plotted point on an **EWMA** chart represent?",options:["The raw measurement taken at that time","The cumulative sum of all deviations from target, each weighted equally","A weighted average of the current and all previous observations, with weights that shrink exponentially for older data","A simple average of the last few observations, all weighted equally"],answer:2,hint:"$z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}$",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "The items plotted are NOT the actual measurements but the weighted measurements." Expanding $z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}$ gives weights $\\lambda$, $\\lambda(1-\\lambda)$, $\\lambda(1-\\lambda)^2$, … on older points.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Raw values are plotted on an I chart; equal weighting of all past deviations is the CuSum; equal weighting of the last few points is a simple moving average."}],final:"Exponentially weighted average → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Raw values are what an I chart plots; the book stresses EWMA points are NOT the actual measurements.","Equal weight for all past deviations from target describes the CuSum.","","A simple moving average gives equal weight to a fixed window; EWMA weights decay and never fully drop old data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 541"},{id:"sv-5-308",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, an EWMA chart can be used for **individual measurements** and also for **averages of subgroups**. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:'EWMA: "most commonly used for individual measurements although has been used for averages of subgroups."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: EWMA is most commonly used for individual measurements, although it has been used for averages of subgroups. $X_t$ is "individual data point or sample averages at time t".'},{title:"Conclude",body:"Both uses are allowed; with subgroups, σ is divided by $\\sqrt{n}$ in the limit formula."}],final:"Both uses are allowed → **True** (answer **True**)",whyWrong:["","The book's EWMA formula includes n (subgroup size) and $X_t$ may be a sample average, so subgroups are allowed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 541"},{id:"sv-5-309",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An I chart of **daily dropped-call count** has a centre line of **40** and a UCL of **49**. Above the centre line, where is the boundary between **zone B and zone A** (the line used by Test 5)?",options:["43","44.5","46","49"],answer:2,hint:"$\\sigma=\\dfrac{UCL-CL}{3}$; zone C/B boundary $=CL+1\\sigma$, zone B/A boundary $=CL+2\\sigma$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"CL = 40, UCL = 49 (that is CL + 3σ)."},{title:"Find sigma",body:"$$\\sigma=\\dfrac{UCL-CL}{3}=\\dfrac{49-40}{3}=\\dfrac{9}{3}=3$$"},{title:"Locate the zone line",body:"$$CL+2\\sigma=40+2\\times 3=46$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Test 5 fails when 2 of 3 consecutive points fall above 46 on the same side."}],final:"Zone B/A boundary = 46 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["CL + 1σ = 43 is the boundary between zone C and zone B.","Took halfway between CL and UCL; the zones are in whole sigmas.","","49 is the UCL (CL + 3σ), the outer edge of zone A."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-310",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Test Results for Xbar Chart of Order Fallout Time (min)

TEST 6. 4 out of 5 points more than 1 standard deviation from
        center line (on one side of CL).
Test Failed at points:  22
\`\`\`
What does this test MOST likely indicate?`,options:["A shift in the process mean, or a worsening of variation","A dramatic improvement in the process variation","A non-random pattern of points alternating up and down","A gradual trend in the mean, with each point higher than the last"],answer:0,hint:"Test 6 (4 of 5 beyond 1σ, same side) → shift in mean or degradation of variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: Test 6 "is indicating a shift in the Mean or degradation of variation". Only the four points beyond 1σ need to be on the same side.'},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Point 22 completes four of five recent points beyond 1σ on one side, so the subgroup means have moved off centre."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Dramatic reduction in variation is Test 7 (15 within 1σ); alternating up and down is Test 4; a steady rise is Test 3."}],final:"Test 6 → shift in mean / worse variation → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","That is Test 7 — 15 points in a row within 1σ.","That is Test 4 — 14 points alternating up and down.","That is Test 3 — 6 points in a row all increasing or all decreasing."],bookRef:"Book p. 537"},{id:"sv-5-311",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:"```\nTest Results for I Chart of Daily Port-in Lead Time (h)\nTEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.\nTest Failed at points:  8, 31\n```\nInvestigation notes: **point 8** — a typing error (48.0 entered as 480); the true value is now confirmed. **Point 31** — a core-router failure; root cause removed and a preventive change is in place. The team now recalculates limits for the control plan. What is the BEST treatment of these two points?",options:["Keep both points in the limit calculation, because all real data must be used","Delete both points from the data and the chart without recording why","Widen the control limits so that values like these no longer fail Test 1","Correct the value at point 8, and exclude point 31 from the limit calculation while keeping it on the chart with a note"],answer:3,hint:"Fix data errors; omit only special-cause points whose cause is found and removed from limit calculations; record actions on the chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Control limits should describe common-cause variation only. The book's monitoring plan asks teams to record actions taken and put notes on the chart."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Point 8 is a measurement/data error: correct it. Point 31 is a real special cause that has been found and removed, so it no longer belongs to the process; exclude it from the limit calculation but leave it visible with its note."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Keeping a removed special cause inflates sigma; deleting points without a record destroys history; widening limits to hide failures defeats SPC."}],final:"Correct 8, exclude 31 from limits and annotate → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Including a special cause that has been removed inflates sigma and makes the new limits too wide.","Silent deletion loses the history the response plan needs (frequency of causes, actions taken).","Control limits come from the data; moving them to avoid signals is not allowed.",""],trap:"Only exclude a point when its special cause has been found and removed.",bookRef:"Book pp. 544, 553, 569"},{id:"sv-5-312",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Lower values are better for **mean time to restore (MTTR)**. Minitab reports:

\`\`\`
Test Results for I Chart of Daily MTTR (h)
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  18   (below LCL)
\`\`\`
What should the team do?`,options:["Ignore it, because a point below the LCL is good news for MTTR","Adjust the process to bring MTTR back to the centre line","Treat it as a special cause: investigate what made day 18 better and, if it is real and repeatable, standardise it","Immediately recalculate the control limits including day 18"],answer:2,hint:"Any signal — good or bad — is a special cause that needs investigating.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Control charts detect special causes, not "bad" points. A signal on the favourable side is still non-random behaviour, and the book says to find the special cause of improvements so they can be sustained.'},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Day 18 is beyond the LCL. Check the measurement first (e.g. tickets closed early by mistake), then learn what was different and build it into the process if genuine."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring wastes a learning chance; pushing MTTR back up is absurd tampering; limits are recalculated after a documented, confirmed change, not after one point."}],final:"Investigate the favourable special cause → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A favourable signal still means something special happened; ignoring it loses the chance to keep the gain (or to find a data error).","Adjusting a process toward the centre line after a favourable point is tampering and would make results worse.","","One point does not justify new limits; limits are recalculated after a confirmed, documented process change."],bookRef:"Book pp. 537, 544, 553"},{id:"sv-5-313",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Two NOC sites send I charts of the same KPI to one monthly report. Site A uses Minitab's default of **9 points in a row** on one side of the centre line; Site B changed it to **7 points in a row**. Managers are confused when the sites disagree about whether the same pattern is a signal. What does the book recommend?",options:["Always switch on all eight tests on every chart so nothing is missed","Decide which special-cause rules the organisation will use, set them in the software, and apply them consistently","Let each site keep its own settings, because the numbers do not matter","Use only Test 1 everywhere, because the other tests are not standard"],answer:1,hint:'Book: organisations differ (7, 8 or 9 in a row) — "Decide, then be consistent when implementing."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that standard rules differ slightly between organisations (e.g. 7 or 8 points instead of 9) and that very few organisations use all eight rules. What matters is consistency."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The organisation should agree one rule set and configure it (Tools > Options > Control Charts and Quality Tools > Tests) at both sites."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"All eight tests raise false alarms and the book says very few use them all; leaving local settings keeps the confusion; dropping to Test 1 throws away useful pattern tests."}],final:"Decide the rules, then be consistent → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book says very few organisations use all eight rules; more tests mean more false alarms.","","Different settings are exactly what produced the conflicting conclusions.","Test 1 alone misses shifts and trends that the run tests catch."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532–533"},{id:"sv-5-314",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A team will start SPC on **hand-drawn charts** at a field depot before any software is available. According to the book, which three special-cause rules are the **easiest for people to spot visually**?",options:["One point beyond 3σ; 6 points in a row increasing or decreasing; 15 points in a row within 1σ","One point beyond 3σ; 2 of 3 points beyond 2σ; 4 of 5 points beyond 1σ","9 points on one side; 14 points alternating; 8 points beyond 1σ on both sides","One point beyond 3σ; 8 points on one side; 2 of 3 points beyond 2σ"],answer:0,hint:"Book: visually easiest = Test 1, 6 increasing/decreasing, 15 within 1σ.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: when SPC is done manually, the three rules most easily detected by personnel are one point beyond 3σ, six points in a row all increasing or decreasing, and fifteen points in a row within 1σ."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Zone-counting rules (2 of 3 beyond 2σ, 4 of 5 beyond 1σ) need sigma lines and careful counting. The set with 8 on one side and 2 of 3 beyond 2σ is part of the Western Electric rules, not the "visually easiest" set.'}],final:"Test 1, 6 trending, 15 within 1σ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Zone tests 5 and 6 need sigma lines and counting, so they are not the visually easy set.","Tests 2, 4 and 8 are long-run patterns that are hard to count by eye.","These are Western Electric rules, a different list in the book."],bookRef:"Book p. 533"},{id:"sv-5-315",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"All of the following are among the four **Western Electric rules** listed in the book EXCEPT:",options:["One point more than 3 standard deviations from the centre line","2 out of 3 points more than 2 standard deviations from the centre line on the same side","4 out of 5 points more than 1 standard deviation from the centre line on the same side","6 points in a row all increasing or all decreasing"],answer:3,hint:"Western Electric: 1 beyond 3σ; 8 on one side; 2 of 3 beyond 2σ; 4 of 5 beyond 1σ.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book credits Shewhart and Western Electric with four rules: one point beyond 3σ, 8 points in a row on one side, 2 of 3 beyond 2σ (same side), 4 of 5 beyond 1σ (same side)."},{title:"Find the odd one out",body:`"6 points in a row increasing or decreasing" (Test 3, a trend rule) is in the book's visually-easy list, not the Western Electric list.`}],final:"Trend rule is not a Western Electric rule → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is a Western Electric rule (and Minitab Test 1).","This is a Western Electric rule (Test 5).","This is a Western Electric rule (Test 6).",""],bookRef:"Book p. 533"},{id:"sv-5-316",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A p chart of failed bill runs signalled; the team followed the OCAP and corrected a rate-table load. The next point is **back inside the limits but still above the centre line**. The supervisor wants one more adjustment "to get it back to average". What does the book advise?',options:["Make no further changes while the process shows in control, even if the point is above the centre line","Make the extra adjustment, because the process is not yet at the centre line","Recalculate the control limits now using the latest point","Stop the bill runs until a point falls exactly on the centre line"],answer:0,hint:"After corrective action: if the next reading is back in control, DO NOT make more changes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: after corrective actions, wait for the next sample. If still out of control, follow the OCAP further. If back in control, "DO NOT make any more changes … even if the next reading seems higher than the Center Line! Do not cause more variability."'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The point is in control. Variation around the centre line is common cause; adjusting for it is tampering."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Recalculating after one point is wrong; waiting for a point exactly on the centre line misunderstands common-cause variation."}],final:"In control → no more adjustments → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Adjusting to common-cause variation (tampering) increases variation.","Limits are recalculated after a documented, confirmed change, not after each new point.","Points are expected to scatter around the centre line; one landing exactly on it is not a requirement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 544, 548"},{id:"sv-5-317",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Eight months after go-live, the I chart of **order-entry time** looks tighter than before, and a test for equal variances confirms that variation has fallen. The control limits are still the ones calculated at handoff. What does the book recommend?",options:["Recalculate the limits automatically after every new point","Never recalculate, because the limits from handoff are the official baseline","Recalculate the centre line and limits for the documented change, and also review limits on a set schedule (e.g. every 3, 6 or 12 months)","Recalculate only when a point falls outside the current limits"],answer:2,hint:"Step 9 of SPC: recalculate after a reduction in variation or known shift (confirmed by hypothesis test) and on a periodic schedule.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: reduction in variation or a known process shift should lead to recalculation; statistical confidence can be confirmed with hypothesis testing; a periodic check (3, 6, 12 months) captures process changes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The reduction is confirmed by a test for equal variances, so new limits should be calculated. The old, wider limits would hide new special causes."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Recalculating after every point lets special causes drift into the limits (the book warns Minitab will do this unless the parameters are fixed); never recalculating keeps outdated limits; a single signal is a reason to investigate, not to reset limits."}],final:"Recalculate for confirmed change and periodically → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Continuous recalculation absorbs special causes into the limits; the book fixes the parameters to prevent this.","Limits that no longer match the improved process cannot detect new special causes.","","A signal calls for the OCAP, not new limits."],bookRef:"Book pp. 547, 553"},{id:"sv-5-318",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`An Xbar chart has CL = **100** and σ (of the plotted means) = **4**, so the zone A lines are at **108** and **92** and the control limits at 112 and 88. Four windows of three consecutive points are shown:

| Window | Point 1 | Point 2 | Point 3 |
|---|---|---|---|
| W1 | 109 | 101 | 110 |
| W2 | 109 | 91 | 104 |
| W3 | 107 | 109 | 106 |
| W4 | 104 | 113 | 103 |

Which window fails **Test 5** (2 out of 3 points more than 2σ from the centre line on the same side)?`,options:["W1","W2","W3","W4"],answer:0,hint:"Test 5: two of three consecutive points beyond 2σ, same side; they need not be consecutive.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The two points beyond 2σ must be on the **same side**, but they do not have to be next to each other."},{title:"Check each window",body:"W1: 109 and 110 are both above 108 → fails Test 5. W2: 109 above and 91 below — opposite sides → no. W3: only 109 is above 108 → no. W4: only one point (113) beyond 2σ; it is beyond the UCL, so it fails Test 1, not Test 5."}],final:"W1 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The two extreme points are on opposite sides of the centre line; Test 5 needs the same side.","Only one of the three points (109) is beyond 108.","113 is a single point beyond 3σ — that is Test 1, and Test 5 needs two points beyond 2σ."],trap:"The two Test-5 points need not be consecutive, but must be on the same side.",bookRef:"Book pp. 536–537"},{id:"sv-5-319",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"If a point on an I chart is **not on the centre line**, the process is showing a sign of being out of control. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Common-cause variation scatters points around the centre line; only rule violations signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "being off the Center Line is NOT a sign of out of control because Common Cause variation exists."'},{title:"Conclude",body:"Only the agreed special-cause tests indicate out of control. Reacting to every off-centre point increases variation."}],final:"Off-centre points are normal → **False** (answer **False**)",whyWrong:["Points naturally scatter around the centre line because of common-cause variation; only a failed test signals.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-5-320",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`After control limits are set, the book's SPC methodology continues with Steps 6–9. Put them in order:

- **P** — Check for out-of-control conditions after each point
- **Q** — Plot the process X or Y on the newly created control chart
- **R** — Recalculate the control limits when a process change is documented
- **S** — Interpret findings, investigate special causes and act following the OCAP`,options:["P → Q → S → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:1,hint:"Plot → check → interpret/OCAP → recalculate (Step 9).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists Step 6 plot, Step 7 check for OOC after each point, Step 8 interpret and investigate using the OCAP, Step 9 recalculate limits."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q → P → S → R."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"You cannot check a point that has not been plotted; recalculation is the last step and follows a documented change, not each investigation."}],final:"Q → P → S → R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A point must be plotted before it can be checked.","","Investigation follows detection, so checking (P) comes before interpreting (S).","Recalculation (Step 9) comes after interpreting and acting on signals (Step 8)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 547, 553"},{id:"sv-5-321",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Rank these controls from **MOST effective** to **LEAST effective**, as in the book's control-methods hierarchy:

- **P** — A standard operating procedure telling staff to check for the error
- **Q** — A design change that makes the error condition impossible (Type 1 countermeasure)
- **R** — 100% inspection of completed orders before release (containment)
- **S** — A system check that detects the error and stops the order from moving on (Type 2 flag)`,options:["S → Q → R → P","Q → R → S → P","Q → S → P → R","Q → S → R → P"],answer:3,hint:"Type 1 prevent → Type 2 flag/stop → SPC with empowered staff → inspection/containment → SOP.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book ranks: Type 1 corrective action (countermeasure — the defect can never be created), Type 2 (flag — detect and shut down), SPC on Xs with trained and empowered staff, Type 3 containment (audits/100% inspection), then an SOP, which "is not sustainable".'},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (Type 1) → S (Type 2) → R (inspection) → P (SOP)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Detection is weaker than prevention; inspection is a short-term containment but still stronger than relying on people to follow an SOP."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Detecting and stopping (Type 2) is weaker than making the error impossible (Type 1).","A flag that stops the defect automatically ranks above 100% inspection.","The SOP is the weakest control in the book's list, below inspection.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-322",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"In a contact centre, control charts of key KPIs are printed every week and pinned on the wall. Nobody has been trained to read them and supervisors are not allowed to change staffing when a chart signals. How does the book describe this use of SPC?",options:["A Type 1 corrective action, because the charts are visible to everyone",'"Wallpaper" — SPC without proper usage, which adds no control',"Visual management that will sustain the gains on its own","A Type 2 flag, because the charts detect problems automatically"],answer:1,hint:"SPC only controls a process when trained, empowered people react with an OCAP.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`The book's control-methods list ends with "SPC on X's or Y's without proper usage = WALL PAPER". SPC is effective only when staff are trained, respect the rules and are empowered to act.`},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Untrained readers and no authority to act means signals are never answered — the charts are decoration."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Type 1 prevents errors by design; Type 2 automatically stops the defect; visible charts alone do not sustain anything."}],final:"Unused charts = wallpaper → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Type 1 is a design change that makes the error impossible; a chart on a wall prevents nothing.","","Visual management needs a defined reaction; without it nothing is sustained.","A Type 2 flag stops the defect from moving on; these charts trigger no action at all."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 544"},{id:"sv-5-323",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A provisioning system checks each order after it is keyed. When it detects a missing port-in PIN, it **blocks the order from moving to the next step** until it is fixed. In the book's control-methods list, what is this?",options:["A Type 1 corrective action (countermeasure)","A Type 3 corrective action (inspection/containment)","A Type 2 corrective action (flag)","An SOP-based control"],answer:2,hint:"Type 1 = error cannot occur; Type 2 = error detected and process stopped; Type 3 = inspection/containment.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Type 1 eliminates the error condition so the defect is never created. Type 2 detects when the error has occurred and shuts the process down so the defect does not move forward. Type 3 is a short-term containment such as audits or 100% inspection."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The error (missing PIN) can still be made, but it is detected and the order is stopped from moving on → Type 2, the detection form of poka-yoke."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A Type 1 control would make it impossible to submit an order without a PIN in the first place."}],final:"Detect and stop = Type 2 flag → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Type 1 would prevent the error from being made at all (e.g. a mandatory field that cannot be skipped).","Type 3 is people inspecting or auditing output; here the system stops the order automatically.","","An SOP relies on people remembering to check; this is an automatic system block."],trap:"Detect-and-stop is Type 2, even when automatic; Type 1 means the error cannot occur.",bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-324",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An automated SIM-swap identity check costs **OMR 20,000 once** to build. It adds **OMR 4,000 a year** in running costs and saves **OMR 30,000 a year** in fraud losses. Ignoring discounting, what is the **net benefit over the first two years** (OMR thousands)?",options:["6","32","40","52"],answer:1,hint:"$$\\text{Net benefit}=\\text{years}\\times(\\text{saving}-\\text{running cost})-\\text{one-time cost}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"One-time 20; running 4 per year; saving 30 per year; 2 years (OMR thousands)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Net}=\\text{years}\\times(\\text{saving}-\\text{running})-\\text{one-time}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Net}=2\\times(30-4)-20=52-20=32$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The control pays back within the first year and gives OMR 32k net over two years — both the initial and the on-going costs had to be counted."}],final:"Net benefit = OMR 32k → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Calculated only one year: 30 − 4 − 20 = 6.","","Ignored the on-going running cost: 2 × 30 − 20 = 40.","Ignored the one-time build cost: 2 × (30 − 4) = 52."],trap:"Count initial AND on-going costs.",bookRef:"Book pp. 557–558"},{id:"sv-5-325",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the Improvement Selection Matrix, a candidate improvement has the **highest overall impact** of all options but receives a **time rating of 0** (more than a year to implement). What happens to it?",options:["Its overall rating becomes 0, which removes it from consideration for the project","It is still ranked first, because impact matters more than time","Its time rating is ignored and only impact and cost are multiplied","It is automatically split into two smaller improvements"],answer:0,hint:"Overall rating = overall impact × cost rating × time rating; a 0 cost or time rating eliminates the option.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: cost and time ratings are multiplied against the overall impact rating, and "a cost or time rating of zero would eliminate the improvement from consideration".'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Any impact × cost × 0 = 0, so the option drops to the bottom however large its impact."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Time is not optional in the product; nothing in the method splits options automatically (though long items may run as separate parallel efforts)."}],final:"Zero rating → eliminated → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The ratings are multiplied, so a zero time rating outweighs any impact.","All three criteria are used; the book multiplies impact by both cost and time.","The matrix rates options as listed; it does not restructure them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 559, 561"},{id:"sv-5-326",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"On the book's impact-rating scale (0–7) for the Improvement Selection Matrix, what rating does an improvement receive if it only ensures that **personnel are trained about the X's impact on the output**?",options:["0","1","4","7"],answer:1,hint:"Impact scale: 7 = X removed; … 4 = defect detection of output; … 1 = personnel trained; 0 = no impact.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's guideline: 7 — the X is removed from impacting the output; 6 — continual control and adjustment of critical Xs; 4 — defect detection prevents unknown defects leaving; 1 — personnel are trained about the X's impact; 0 — no impact."},{title:"Apply",body:"Training alone is the weakest form of impact that still counts → 1."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"This matches the book's impact list, where removing the root cause is most effective and training people is least effective."}],final:"Training only → impact rating 1 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0 means the improvement has no impact on the output at all; training has some (small) impact.","","4 is for defect detection of the output, which stops unknown defects leaving the process.","7 is for removing the X from impacting the output — the root-cause fix."],bookRef:"Book pp. 557, 560"},{id:"sv-5-327",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A team rates the **time to implement** for an automated address-validation rule. The build takes **10 days**, but the change-approval board needs about **6 more weeks** before it can go live. Using the book's 0–7 time scale, which rating applies?",options:["4","5","6","7"],answer:1,hint:"Time scale: 7 < 1 week; 6 = 7–14 days; 5 = 2–8 weeks; 4 = 2–3 months … All times include time for the approvals process.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Build ≈ 10 days; approvals ≈ 6 weeks; total ≈ 7–8 weeks."},{title:"Apply the scale",body:"The book says the ratings include time for approvals. About 7.5 weeks falls in the 2–8 week band → rating 5."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Rating 6 uses the build time only; rating 4 (2–3 months) overstates the total."}],final:"About 7.5 weeks including approvals → rating 5 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["2–3 months overstates a total of about 7.5 weeks.","","Used only the 10-day build time and left out the approvals, which the book includes.","Less than a week does not fit even the build time."],trap:"Time ratings include the approvals process.",bookRef:"Book p. 561"},{id:"sv-5-328",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A control for duplicate-order errors can be **expensed** — no capital authorisation is needed — and its **recurring costs are low**, though not minimal. Using the book's 0–7 cost-to-implement scale, which rating fits BEST?",options:["4","5","6","7"],answer:2,hint:"Cost scale: 7 minimal upfront and ongoing; 6 low, expensed, no capital, low recurring; 5 same but higher recurring; 4 medium capital priority.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's cost ratings: 7 — costs minimal upfront and ongoing; 6 — low, can be expensed with no capital authorisation and recurring expenses are low; 5 — as 6 but recurring expenses higher; 4 — medium capital priority."},{title:"Apply",body:"Expensed, no capital, low recurring → 6. Higher ratings mean cheaper options and therefore a higher priority score."}],final:"Expensed, no capital, low recurring → rating 6 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["4 applies when capital spend is needed (medium capital priority).","5 is for expensed improvements whose recurring costs are higher.","","7 needs minimal upfront AND ongoing cost; the recurring cost here is low but not minimal."],bookRef:"Book p. 560"},{id:"sv-5-329",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"When the cost of an improvement is estimated, which of the following is an **on-going** cost rather than an initial cost?",options:["Capital purchase of new test equipment","Training the existing workforce when the change goes live","Staff time used to build the improvement","Future refresher training, inspection and monitoring"],answer:3,hint:"Initial costs: train existing workforce, new materials, build resources, capital. On-going: future training, inspection, monitoring, materials.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book splits cost into initial costs (training the existing workforce, new materials, resources to build, capital) and on-going costs to sustain the improvement (future training, inspection, monitoring and material costs)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Refresher training and continuing inspection or monitoring recur for as long as the control runs → on-going."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three are one-time costs paid to put the improvement in place."}],final:"Future training/inspection/monitoring = on-going → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Capital investment is an initial cost.","Training the existing workforce at launch is an initial cost; only future training is on-going.","Resources used to build the improvement are an initial cost.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 558"},{id:"sv-5-330",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A small Green Belt project ends with only **two** simple controls: a mandatory field in the order form and a weekly p chart. The sponsor asks whether the team must complete a full Improvement Selection Matrix. What does the book suggest?",options:["Yes — the matrix is mandatory for every Six Sigma project","Yes — but only the impact ratings need to be filled in","No — the selection tool is unnecessary for just a few changes; a simple cost-benefit check is enough","No — cost and time never matter for small projects"],answer:2,hint:'Book: "The tool for selecting Defect Prevention methods is unnecessary for just a few changes to the process."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the selection tool is unnecessary for just a few changes; many smaller-scope projects have few but vital control methods. It is best used when multiple improvement efforts are considered."},{title:"Apply",body:"Two controls do not need ranking; a cost-benefit check is still a good tool for setting priorities."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The matrix is not compulsory; cost and time still matter even when the formal matrix is skipped."}],final:"Matrix not needed for a few changes → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The book says the matrix is for choosing among many options, not a mandatory form.","Rating impact alone ignores cost and time and is not what the book suggests.","","Cost and time always matter; only the formal matrix is unnecessary."],bookRef:"Book p. 557"},{id:"sv-5-331",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`An Improvement Selection Matrix for provisioning errors gives (overall impact already weighted by output significance; cost and time rated 0–7, 7 = best):

| Option | Overall impact | Cost rating | Time rating |
|---|---|---|---|
| A — Address auto-validation | 120 | 5 | 6 |
| B — New OSS module | 180 | 0 | 2 |
| C — Agent refresher training | 60 | 7 | 7 |
| D — Supervisor daily audit of orders | 90 | 6 | 7 |

Which option should get **first priority**?`,options:["A","B","C","D"],answer:3,hint:"Overall rating = overall impact × cost rating × time rating; highest first.",steps:[{title:"Calculate each overall rating",body:"A: $120\\times 5\\times 6=3600$. B: $180\\times 0\\times 2=0$. C: $60\\times 7\\times 7=2940$. D: $90\\times 6\\times 7=3780$."},{title:"Decide",body:"D has the highest overall rating (3,780), so it gets first priority; A is second."},{title:"Watch the trap",body:"B has the biggest impact, but a cost rating of 0 eliminates it."}],final:"D = 3,780 is highest → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A (3,600) is a close second, but D is higher.","Highest impact, but the cost rating of 0 makes its overall rating 0 — eliminated.","Best cost and time ratings, but its low impact gives only 2,940.",""],trap:"Rank by the product, not by impact alone.",bookRef:"Book pp. 559–561"},{id:"sv-5-332",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`To validate the claimed saving, the team compares **cost per order (OMR)** before and after the improvement:

\`\`\`
Two-Sample T-Test and CI: Before, After
Difference = mu (Before) - mu (After)
Estimate for difference:  1.84
95% CI for difference:  (0.71, 2.97)
T-Test of difference = 0 (vs not =): T-Value = 3.27  P-Value = 0.002  DF = 46
\`\`\`
With α = 0.05, which statement is correct?`,options:["Fail to reject H₀: the cost difference is not statistically significant","Reject H₀: the after-improvement mean cost is lower, by about OMR 0.71–2.97 per order with 95% confidence","Accept H₀: the means are equal, so no saving can be claimed","The test proves every future order will cost exactly OMR 1.84 less"],answer:1,hint:"p < α → reject H₀; a CI that excludes 0 agrees.",steps:[{title:"Read the right numbers",body:"P-Value = 0.002 and the 95% CI for the difference is (0.71, 2.97)."},{title:"Decide",body:"0.002 < 0.05 → reject H₀. The CI does not contain 0 and is entirely positive, so Before is higher than After: the saving is real."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'"Fail to reject" is the wrong decision here; "accept H₀" is never the wording; 1.84 is an estimate of the mean difference, not a guarantee for each order.'}],final:"p = 0.002 < 0.05 → reject H₀, saving confirmed → answer **B**",whyWrong:["p = 0.002 is below 0.05, so H₀ is rejected, not retained.","",'H₀ is never "accepted", and here it is rejected anyway.',"1.84 is a point estimate of the average difference; the CI shows uncertainty and individual orders vary."],bookRef:"Book pp. 553, 577–578"},{id:"sv-5-333",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The book lists technical, political and cultural **time constraints** for implementing an improvement. Which question describes a **cultural** time constraint?",options:["How long will it take to gain support from the necessary stakeholders?","What is the minimum time needed to build and implement the improvement?","What other priorities are competing for the technical time to build it?","How long will the capital approval and purchasing cycle take?"],answer:0,hint:"Technical = minimum build/implement time; political = competing priorities; cultural = time to gain stakeholder support.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: technical constraints — the minimum time to build and implement; political constraints — other priorities competing for the technical time; cultural constraints — how long it will take to gain support from necessary stakeholders."},{title:"Apply",body:"Gaining stakeholder support is a cultural constraint."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Competing priorities sound "cultural" but the book calls them political; build time is technical; capital approval time is a cost/approval matter that the time rating already includes.'}],final:"Stakeholder support = cultural → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Minimum build and implementation time is the technical constraint.","Competing priorities for technical time is the book's political constraint.","Approval cycles are counted inside the time rating; they are not the book's definition of a cultural constraint."],trap:"Competing priorities = political, not cultural.",bookRef:"Book p. 558"},{id:"sv-5-334",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, **who** should create the control plan?",options:["The Green Belt alone, because the Belt knows the data best","The quality department, after the project team has left","The Champion, who signs it off without team input","The project team, together with anyone who has a role in defining, executing or changing the process"],answer:3,hint:'Book: "The team working on the project!!!!" plus anyone who defines, executes or changes the process.',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the control plan is created by the team working on the project, and by anyone with a role in defining, executing or changing the process — associates, technical experts, supervisors, managers, site manager, HR."},{title:"Apply",body:'Involving these people builds ownership and makes the plan workable after handoff; the book lists "team members not involved in Control Plan design" as a roadblock.'}],final:"Team plus everyone who touches the process → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A Belt working alone creates a plan nobody owns — a roadblock the book names.","Handing it to another department after the team leaves loses process knowledge and ownership.","Sign-off is important, but the Champion does not write the plan alone.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 563, 577"},{id:"sv-5-335",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:`A regional manager says: "We don't need a control plan for the fibre-fault process — Khalid always fixes it when the backlog builds up." Which reason from the book BEST answers this?`,options:["A control plan removes the need for constant heroes who repeatedly solve the same problems","A control plan is only required when a customer demands it","A control plan replaces the detailed operating procedures Khalid uses","A control plan is mainly a record for the Belt's certification"],answer:0,hint:"Why a control plan: sustain results, let the Belt move on, prevent the need for constant heroes, increasingly a customer requirement.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`The book's "Why do we need a Control Plan?": results must be sustained; it lets the Belt move on; it "prevents need for constant heroes in an organization who repeatedly (seem to) solve the same problems"; it is increasingly a customer requirement.`},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'Depending on one person fixing the same problem again and again is the "hero" pattern a control plan is meant to remove.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Customer demand is one reason, not the only trigger; a control plan does not replace SOPs; it is not a certification record."}],final:"No more firefighting heroes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Customer demand is one reason among several; the plan is needed to sustain results anyway.","The book says a control plan is NOT a replacement for detailed operating instructions.","The plan exists to sustain the process, not to support anyone's certification."],bookRef:"Book pp. 562–563"},{id:"sv-5-336",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"At handoff the Green Belt confirms that all SOPs and process maps are up to date. Nobody is named to **update** them when the process changes or to **review** them on a regular basis. Which control-plan element is incomplete?",options:["The training plan — schedule for new-employee training","The response plan — triggers for out-of-control points","The documentation plan — ongoing responsibility for updates and reviews","Aligning systems and structures — incentive compensation"],answer:2,hint:"Documentation plan: Belt ensures docs current at handoff AND that processes exist to modify and review them; plan names who updates and who reviews.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: at implementation the Belt makes sure documents are current at handoff, that there is a process to modify them as the process changes, and that there is a process to review them regularly. For the ongoing process the plan must outline who updates and who reviews."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Current documents exist, but ownership of updates and reviews is missing → the documentation plan is incomplete."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The gap is not about training schedules, chart triggers or incentives."}],final:"Documentation plan responsibilities missing → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Training schedules are a different element; the gap here is keeping documents current.","Response-plan triggers deal with signals from monitoring, not document ownership.","","Incentives belong to aligning systems and structures; the stem is about document updates and reviews."],bookRef:"Book pp. 566–568"},{id:"sv-5-337",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After handoff, who does the book say is responsible for **adherence to the monitoring plan** in day-to-day operations?",options:["The Green Belt, for as long as the process runs","Functional managers, who must be trained to do it and held accountable","The finance department, because it tracks the savings","An external auditor, appointed once a year"],answer:1,hint:"Monitoring plan: Belt develops it; functional managers sustain it (trained and accountable).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: the Belt develops the monitoring plan with the team and stakeholders, but to sustain it "functional managers will be responsible for adherence to the monitoring plan — they must be trained on how to do this; they must be made accountable for adherence."'},{title:"Apply",body:"The day-to-day owner is the line (functional) manager, not the Belt who has moved on."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Finance may track results, and audits may help, but neither is accountable for daily adherence."}],final:"Functional managers → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The Belt develops the plan, but one purpose of the control plan is to let the Belt move on.","","Finance tracks financial results; it does not run the monitoring in the process.","A yearly audit cannot provide day-to-day adherence."],bookRef:"Book pp. 563, 568"},{id:"sv-5-338",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The book's documentation plan lists items under several headings. Which items are listed under **systems and structures**?",options:["Updated process maps and SOPs","Process management charts and reports","The FMEA used to drive the response plan","Job descriptions and performance management objectives"],answer:3,hint:"Documentation plan: process docs (maps, SOPs, FMEA); control plan docs — training manuals, monitoring plan (charts, reports, SOPs), response plan (FMEA), systems & structures (job descriptions, performance objectives).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Documentation plan items: process documentation (updated maps, SOPs, FMEA) and control plan documentation — training manuals; monitoring plan (process management charts, reports, SOPs); response plan (FMEA); systems and structures (job descriptions, performance management objectives)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Systems and structures are documented through job descriptions and performance objectives — the things that make the new behaviour part of people's roles."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Maps and SOPs are process documentation; charts and reports belong to the monitoring plan; FMEA supports the response plan."}],final:"Job descriptions and performance objectives → answer **D**",whyWrong:["These are process documentation items.","These belong to the monitoring plan documentation.","The FMEA is listed with process documentation and the response plan.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 567, 572"},{id:"sv-5-339",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"After a project reduced repeat repairs, field technicians are still paid a bonus per **job closed**. Closures are rising again and repeat faults are creeping back. Which control-plan element should the team address?",options:["Training plan — schedule a refresher on the repair SOP","Aligning systems and structures — remove measures and incentives that reward behaviour opposed to the new process","Monitoring plan — sample repairs more often","Documentation plan — reprint the updated process map"],answer:1,hint:"Systems and structures (goals, policies, job descriptions, incentives) let people change behaviour permanently; get rid of measures not aligned with desired behaviours.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: systems and structures are the basis for allowing people to change their behaviours permanently — performance goals, policies, job descriptions, incentive compensation. "Get rid of measurements not aligned with desired behaviors"; ask whether incentives are hurting your chance of success.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'A per-closure bonus rewards fast, incomplete fixes, which works against "fix it right first time". The incentive must be realigned (e.g. reward first-time fix).'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"More training, sampling or documents will not beat an incentive that pays for the old behaviour."}],final:"Misaligned incentive → align systems and structures → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Technicians know the SOP; the bonus pays them to work against it.","","More monitoring will only measure the problem more often.","A reprinted map does not change what people are paid to do."],bookRef:"Book pp. 572–573"},{id:"sv-5-340",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"All of the following are strategies the book gives for **aligning systems and structures** EXCEPT:",options:["Get rid of measurements not aligned with desired behaviours","Implement measures aligned with desired behaviours that incentives do not yet motivate","Add several overlapping measures for the same desired behaviour so it is checked from every angle","Consider whether hourly incentives are hurting the chance of success"],answer:2,hint:"Book: remove misaligned measures; remove multiple measures for the same behaviour; add aligned measures; check incentives.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's alignment strategies: get rid of measurements not aligned with desired behaviours; get rid of multiple measures for the same desired behaviours; implement measures aligned with desired behaviours not yet motivated by incentives; consider how change management and incentives affect success."},{title:"Find the odd one out",body:"Adding several overlapping measures for the same behaviour is the opposite of the book's advice to remove duplicate measures."}],final:"Duplicate measures should be removed, not added → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is one of the book's strategies.","This is one of the book's strategies.","",'The book asks this question directly ("Are the hourly incentives hurting your chance of success?").'],bookRef:"Book p. 573"},{id:"sv-5-341",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"The team is ready to close the project. According to the book, what is the BEST way to secure acceptance of the control plan?",options:["A sign-off meeting with the area's supervisors and management: summary report, specific process changes highlighted and where the control plan is filed","E-mail the control plan to the process owner and close the project","Upload the control plan to a shared folder and announce it in a newsletter","Ask the Master Black Belt to approve it on the team's behalf"],answer:0,hint:"Project sign-off: supervisors and management of the area; meeting with summary report, changes highlighted, filing location.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "Best method to assure acceptance of Control Plan is having supervisors and management for the area involved" — a summary-report meeting, specific process changes highlighted, and information on where the plan is filed.'},{title:"Apply",body:'A face-to-face sign-off with the people who will run the process creates ownership; "lack of project sign off" is a listed Control-phase roadblock.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Sending or filing a document without discussion does not transfer ownership; a coach's approval is no substitute for the area's management."}],final:"Sign-off meeting with area management → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","An e-mail without a meeting does not secure acceptance or explain the changes.","Publishing the file is not the same as the area accepting and owning it.","The people who must run the process — the area's supervisors and managers — need to sign off."],bookRef:"Book pp. 573, 577"},{id:"sv-5-342",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of the following is a source of information the book says the team should use to **develop the control plan**?",options:["The personal opinions of the most senior manager only","Benchmark data from an unrelated industry, in place of process data","The original project charter's timeline","Results from the Measure and Analyze phases"],answer:3,hint:"Control plan inputs: Measure/Analyze results, lessons learned, team knowledge, design FMEAs, design reviews, defect prevention methods selected.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the team develops the control plan using all available information — results from the Measure and Analyze phases, lessons learned from similar products and processes, the team's process knowledge, design FMEAs, design reviews and the defect prevention methods selected."},{title:"Apply",body:"Measure and Analyze results tell the team which Xs are critical and how they should be controlled."}],final:"Measure and Analyze results → answer **D**",whyWrong:["One person's opinion is not in the book's list; the plan draws on team knowledge and data.","Unrelated benchmarks cannot replace the project's own data.","The charter timeline does not say what to monitor or how to react.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 564"},{id:"sv-5-343",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'The process FMEA rated "wrong customer address" with Occurrence 7 and Detection 6. A **mandatory address-validation rule** is now live. What should happen to the FMEA within the control plan?',options:["Freeze the FMEA as it was at the end of Analyze, as a historical record","Delete this failure mode from the FMEA because it has been solved","Reassess the ratings and RPN for this failure mode to reflect the new control, and keep the FMEA current as the process changes","Leave the scores unchanged until the annual audit"],answer:2,hint:"FMEA in the monitoring/response plan: keeps the document current — reassess RPNs as the process changes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: the FMEA is a great tool for the monitoring plan; it "provides the means to keep the document current — reassessing RPNs as the process changes". The FMEA is also listed in the documentation and response plans.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'The new rule lowers occurrence and/or improves detection, so the ratings and RPN should be re-scored and the "actions taken" columns completed.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Freezing or deleting the failure mode loses the risk history and the reason the control exists; waiting a year leaves the plan out of date."}],final:"Re-score and keep the FMEA current → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A frozen FMEA cannot guide monitoring or response as the process changes.","The failure mode can still happen if the control fails, so it must stay visible.","","Control-plan documents must be kept current to be useful; a year's delay is too long."],bookRef:"Book pp. 567, 570"},{id:"sv-5-344",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of the following is an example of the **visual management** element of a monitoring plan?",options:["A monthly written report e-mailed to senior management","A red/yellow/green board that changes colour when queue length passes agreed thresholds","A spreadsheet of raw data on a shared drive","An annual customer survey"],answer:1,hint:"Visual management: alerts or signals that trigger action — empty bins, red/yellow/green reports, audible alarms; 5S is necessary.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: visual management uses alerts or signals to trigger action — empty bins returned to show stock is needed, red/yellow/green reports to show process performance, or audible signals. 5S is necessary for visual management."},{title:"Apply",body:"A colour board that changes at agreed thresholds is a signal people can see at a glance and act on."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reports, spreadsheets and surveys contain information but give no immediate signal at the point of work."}],final:"Red/yellow/green signal → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A monthly report is too slow and too remote to trigger action at the workplace.","","Raw data on a drive is not a visible signal.","An annual survey is far too slow to trigger any immediate reaction."],bookRef:"Book p. 570"},{id:"sv-5-345",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:`A new **identity-verification script** has been introduced for retail agents. The monitoring plan must check that agents still know and use it. According to the book's "knowledge tests", when should these checks be done?`,options:["After training, then at regular intervals, plus random checks in an audit style","Only once, at the end of the training session","Only when a customer complaint arrives","Only at the annual performance review"],answer:0,hint:"Knowledge tests — when to sample: after training; regular intervals; random intervals (auditing sense).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's monitoring plan includes knowledge tests: when to sample (after training, at regular intervals, at random intervals in an auditing sense), how to sample and how to measure."},{title:"Apply",body:"Checking right after training confirms learning; regular and random checks show whether the behaviour lasts."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A single test cannot show the behaviour is sustained; waiting for complaints is reactive; an annual review is too infrequent."}],final:"After training + regular + random → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","One test right after training shows learning, not whether the behaviour lasts.","Waiting for complaints means defects have already reached customers.","Once a year is far too infrequent to sustain a new behaviour."],bookRef:"Book p. 569"},{id:"sv-5-346",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The control plan needs the limits for the improved process. Minitab, run with the **Stages** option at the go-live date, shows:

\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Activation Time (min) by Stage
Stage          Xbar     UCL      LCL
Before        42.6     58.1     27.1
After         31.4     37.9     24.9

Test Results for I Chart (stage After)
 * No points failed any test.
\`\`\`
Which limits should the control plan use for ongoing monitoring?`,options:["UCL 58.1 and LCL 27.1 from the Before stage","Limits recalculated from all the data combined across both stages","The customer specification limits, now that the process is improved","UCL 37.9 and LCL 24.9 from the After stage"],answer:3,hint:"After a documented, confirmed change, monitor with limits calculated from the new (stable) stage only.",steps:[{title:"Read the right numbers",body:"The After stage is stable (no tests failed) with CL 31.4, UCL 37.9, LCL 24.9."},{title:"Decide",body:"The process has changed by a documented improvement, so the control plan should monitor the new process with the After-stage limits. The book: a known process shift should result in recalculating the centre line and limits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Before-stage limits are far too wide to catch a return to the old level quickly; combining stages mixes two processes; specifications never replace control limits."}],final:"Use the After-stage limits → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The old limits describe a process that no longer exists; a slide back toward 42 could go undetected for a long time.","Pooling both stages mixes two different processes and inflates the limits.","Specification limits are the voice of the customer; control limits must come from the process.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 524, 553, 569"},{id:"sv-5-347",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A year after closure, repeat-visit rates are creeping up again. The new process owner asks where to start. Where does the book say the control plan should be, and how does it help here?",options:["It is filed as part of the organisation's project-tracking mechanism, both to track benefits and as the reference for unsustained results","It is kept only on the Belt's laptop, so the Belt must be contacted","It is archived and closed at project sign-off, so a new project must start from scratch","It is attached to the project charter, which defines the controls"],answer:0,hint:"Control plan: essential part of the final project report, filed in the project-tracking mechanism — track benefits; reference for unsustained results.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the control plan is an essential portion of the final project report, filed as part of the organisation's project-tracking mechanism to track benefits and to serve as a reference for unsustained results."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The owner should pull the filed plan to see which Xs were controlled, how they were monitored and what the response was meant to be, and check which part has slipped."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A plan held by one person, or closed at sign-off, cannot support the process; the charter defines the problem, not the controls."}],final:"Filed for tracking and as reference for unsustained results → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A plan only the Belt can reach defeats the purpose of letting the Belt move on.","The control plan is a living document; it is not closed at sign-off.","The charter defines the problem and scope, not the final controls."],bookRef:"Book pp. 562, 573"},{id:"sv-5-348",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"The project team delivered the first round of training on the new order-entry process. Six months later, **who** should be running the **ongoing** training for new joiners and refreshers?",options:["The original project team, for as long as the process exists","Each new joiner, by reading the SOP alone","Qualified trainers, typically owned by a training department or the process owner, named in the training plan","The customer, through feedback on errors"],answer:2,hint:"Training plan: immediate training — project team; ongoing training — qualified trainers (training dept or process owner) who must be identified.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: immediate training is planned, developed and run by the project team; ongoing training is typically owned by a training department or process owner, and those responsible for it must be identified."},{title:"Apply",body:"Six months on, ownership belongs to the qualified trainers named in the training plan, with triggers such as new-employee orientation and refreshers."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The project team moves on; reading an SOP alone is not a training plan; customers are not trainers."}],final:"Qualified trainers named in the plan → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The project team handles immediate training only; the Belt and team move on.","Self-study of an SOP is not structured training, and the plan must name who trains.","","Customer feedback is a lagging signal, not a training method."],bookRef:"Book pp. 565–566"},{id:"sv-5-349",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which practice for control charts in the monitoring plan follows the book's guidance?",options:["Keep charts in the quality office and plot a month of data at a time","Post the chart where the data are collected, plot points in real time and write notes on the chart about events affecting performance","Plot points without notes so the chart stays uncluttered","Update the chart only when a customer complaint is received"],answer:1,hint:"Monitoring plan SPC: charts posted where data collected; plot in real time; act per OCAP; record actions; notes encouraged.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's monitoring-plan SPC guidance: post control charts in the area where data are collected, plot points in real time, act on signals using the OCAP, record actions taken, encourage notes on the chart, and set new limits when performance changes."},{title:"Apply",body:"Real-time charts at the workplace, with notes, let people react quickly and later trace causes."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Monthly batch plotting is too slow to react; leaving out notes loses the history of causes and actions; complaint-driven updating makes the chart reactive."}],final:"Posted, real-time, annotated → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Plotting a month at a time removes the chance to react immediately to a signal.","","The book encourages notes; they record causes and actions.","Control charts are updated as data arrive, not when complaints come in."],bookRef:"Book p. 569"},{id:"sv-5-350",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book's diagram of control plan elements, who is shown as **accountable for maintaining the new level of process performance**?",options:["The Green Belt who led the project","The Master Black Belt","The finance department","The process owners"],answer:3,hint:'Control plan figure: "Process owners are accountable to maintain new level of process performance."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's control-plan figure says that process owners are accountable for maintaining the new level of process performance, alongside verified financial impact and implemented improvements."},{title:"Apply",body:"The control plan is handed off so the Belt can move on; accountability stays with the people who own the process."}],final:"Process owners → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The Belt hands the plan off and moves to the next project.","A Master Black Belt coaches; it does not own the process.","Finance validates benefits but does not run the process.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 563–564"},{id:"sv-5-351",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`The control plan's monitoring step produces:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Failed eSIM Downloads
P-bar = 0.042   UCL = 0.069   LCL = 0.015

Test Results for P Chart of Failed eSIM Downloads
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  37
\`\`\`
The response plan lists "p chart Test 1" as a trigger, names the platform lead as responder and gives a checklist (profile server, certificate expiry, carrier API). What should happen next?`,options:["Wait for a second point beyond the UCL to confirm the signal before acting","The platform lead follows the listed response for this trigger and documents the conditions, cause and actions in an abnormality report","Recalculate the p chart limits including point 37","Escalate to the Green Belt who ran the original project to decide what to do"],answer:1,hint:"Response plan components: trigger → recommended response → named responsibility → documentation of the response → detailed conditions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: a response plan contains the triggers, the recommended response for each failure mode, the responsibilities, documentation that the plan was followed, and detailed information on the conditions surrounding the failure mode."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Point 37 fails Test 1, a listed trigger. The named responder works through the agreed response and records what was found and done."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"One point beyond 3σ is enough — the book says you do not need a second point; limits are not recalculated on a signal; the Belt has handed the process off."}],final:"Named responder acts and documents → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book: one point beyond 3σ indicates a special cause; there is no need to confirm with another point before acting.","","Adding a special-cause point to the limits hides the problem; recalculation follows documented process changes.","The response plan already names the responder; handing back to the Belt defeats the handoff."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534, 571–572"},{id:"sv-5-352",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:'```\nTest Results for I Chart of Daily Install No-Access Rate (%)\nTEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.\nTest Failed at points:  52\n```\nNo point is beyond the control limits. The response plan lists "out-of-control point" as a trigger. Should the response plan be activated?',options:["No — only a point beyond a control limit can trigger the response plan","No — Test 2 applies only to attribute charts","Yes — non-random behaviour within the control limits is also a trigger for the response plan","Only if the points are also outside the customer specification"],answer:2,hint:"Response-plan triggers include out-of-control points AND non-random behaviour within control limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists response-plan triggers: an out-of-control point; non-random behaviour within the control limits; a condition proven to produce defects; a check-sheet failure; an automation failure."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"Nine points on one side is non-random behaviour — a probable shift in the mean — even though every point is inside the limits. The response plan should be triggered."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Test 2 applies to both variables and attribute charts; specifications do not decide whether a special cause exists."}],final:"Non-random pattern inside limits is a trigger → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The book lists non-random behaviour within the limits as a separate trigger.","Test 2 is one of the four tests valid for both attribute and variables data.","","Special-cause signals are judged against control limits and tests, not specifications."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532, 535, 571"},{id:"sv-5-353",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`After six months the abnormality reports for **failed fibre activations** are summarised by cause code:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Cause Code (abnormality reports, n = 60)
Cause code          Count   Percent   Cum %
ONT not provisioned   27      45.0     45.0
Wrong port assigned   15      25.0     70.0
Customer absent        9      15.0     85.0
Other                  9      15.0    100.0
\`\`\`
How should the team use this output?`,options:['Target permanent prevention at "ONT not provisioned" first, and update the response plan and FMEA with what was learned',"Nothing — the abnormality reports exist only to prove the response plan was followed","Spread effort equally across all four codes, because every failure matters",'Start with "Other", because unknown causes are the most dangerous'],answer:0,hint:"Abnormality reports give the frequency of corrective actions and feed continuous improvement; the response plan is a living document.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the abnormality report gives detailed documentation, provides a method for ongoing continuous improvement, and is a method to collect the frequency of corrective actions. Response plans are living documents updated with new information."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:'"ONT not provisioned" accounts for 45% of the reports. A permanent fix (ideally Type 1 prevention) there removes the most responses. Feed the learning back into the response plan and FMEA.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'The reports are not only compliance evidence; equal effort ignores the Pareto principle; "Other" is a mixed bag that should be broken down, not targeted first.'}],final:"Fix the top cause permanently and update the plans → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book says the reports provide a method for ongoing improvement and for counting corrective actions, not just compliance.","Equal effort ignores that one cause makes up 45% of the responses.",'"Other" is a catch-all group; it should be split into real causes, not targeted as one cause.'],bookRef:"Book pp. 571–572"},{id:"sv-5-354",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:'The control plan for a data-centre change process includes a **pre-change check sheet**. Tonight an engineer starts a change without completing the "rollback tested" item. No outage has happened yet, and no chart has signalled. What should happen?',options:["Nothing until a control chart signals","Nothing, because no customer has been affected","The engineer should complete the item next week before the monthly review","The response plan is triggered, because a check-sheet failure is one of its listed triggers"],answer:3,hint:"Response-plan triggers: OOC point; non-random pattern; condition proven to produce defects; check-sheet failure; automation failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book lists "check sheet failure" as a trigger for the response plan, alongside chart signals and automation failures.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The check sheet guards a critical X (a tested rollback). Missing it is a trigger now, before any outage."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Waiting for a chart or a customer impact turns a preventive control into a reactive one."}],final:"Check-sheet failure triggers the response → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Charts are only one of several triggers; a check-sheet failure is enough.","The response plan reacts to failed controls before customers are hurt.","Delaying the response leaves tonight's change unprotected.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 571"},{id:"sv-5-355",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are purposes of the **abnormality report** in the book's response plan EXCEPT:",options:["Providing detailed documentation when failure modes occur","Providing a method to collect the frequency of corrective actions","Identifying which operator to discipline for each failure","Fitting with the ISO 9000 idea of a Corrective Action Request (CAR)"],answer:2,hint:"Abnormality report: detailed documentation; ongoing continuous improvement; reinforce commitment to eliminating defects; fits ISO 9000 CAR; frequency of corrective actions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the abnormality report gives detailed documentation when failure modes occur, provides a method for ongoing continuous improvement, reinforces commitment to eliminating defects, fits the ISO 9000 standard of a CAR, and is a method to collect the frequency of corrective actions."},{title:"Find the odd one out",body:"Blaming individuals is not a purpose. The report looks for causes in the process; a blame culture makes people hide problems."}],final:"Blame is not a purpose → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is a listed purpose.","This is a listed purpose.","","This is a listed purpose."],bookRef:"Book p. 572"},{id:"sv-5-356",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The response plan cannot react to everything. Which failure modes does the book say the response-plan **triggers** usually focus on?",options:["Every output measure in the company's KPI dashboard","The highest-risk Xs in the process, identified through the FMEA","Only the failure modes customers have complained about this year","The failure modes with the lowest RPN, because they are easiest to fix"],answer:1,hint:`Triggers: "What are the Failure Modes to check for? Usually monitor the highest risk X's in the process."`,steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`The book's response-plan components start with the triggers: "What are the Failure Modes to check for? Usually monitor the highest risk X's in the process." The FMEA is the tool used to rank that risk.`},{title:"Apply",body:"The response plan follows the risk ranking: high-RPN Xs get monitored triggers and pre-agreed responses."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Monitoring every KPI dilutes attention; complaints are lagging signals; the lowest RPNs are the least important."}],final:"Highest-risk Xs → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Watching every KPI spreads effort thin and ignores the Y = f(X) focus on critical inputs.","","Complaints are a late, partial signal; the FMEA risk ranking is broader.","Low RPN means low risk — the opposite of where triggers should focus."],bookRef:"Book pp. 570–571"},{id:"sv-5-357",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Analysis proved that SIM cards from supplier **lot 7B** cause activation failures. A shipment from lot 7B has just arrived at the warehouse. No activation failures have been recorded yet. What should the response plan require?",options:["Activate the response now, because a condition proven to produce defects is present","Wait until the p chart of activation failures signals","Issue the SIMs and track complaints closely","Recalculate the p chart limits to allow for the weaker lot"],answer:0,hint:'Trigger: "Condition/variable proven to produce defects present."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book lists "condition/variable proven to produce defects present" as a response-plan trigger. Controlling the X means reacting to the input before the output fails.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Lot 7B is a known defect-producing input, so the pre-agreed response (e.g. quarantine and supplier return) should start now."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Waiting for the chart or complaints lets defects reach customers; changing limits to fit a bad input hides the problem."}],final:"Known bad input present → respond now → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Waiting for the output chart means customers suffer first; the X itself is the trigger.","Tracking complaints is reacting after the defect has reached customers.","Changing limits to absorb a known bad input hides a special cause."],bookRef:"Book pp. 526, 571"},{id:"sv-5-358",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the sections of the book's **abnormality report** in the order they are completed:

- **P** — Root-cause analysis, with dates for completing the analysis and implementing permanent prevention
- **Q** — Current situation: the metric, the signal and the date
- **R** — Corrective action: what was done and who was involved
- **S** — Investigation of cause and the cause code`,options:["Q → R → S → P","S → Q → R → P","Q → S → P → R","Q → S → R → P"],answer:3,hint:"Abnormality report: Current situation → Investigation of cause → Corrective action → Root-cause analysis and permanent prevention.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's abnormality report runs: current situation (process, metric, signal, situation code, details, date) → investigation of cause (code of cause) → corrective action (who is involved) → root-cause analysis (what to be done, date for analysis, date for permanent prevention)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q → S → R → P."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"You cannot investigate a signal that has not been described; permanent prevention follows the immediate corrective action."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Corrective action needs the investigation of cause first.","The situation and signal are recorded before the cause can be investigated.","Immediate corrective action comes before root-cause analysis and permanent prevention.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 572"},{id:"sv-5-359",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"While responding to a signal on the chart of **failed number ports**, the on-call engineer finds a new cause: a partner operator changed its API without notice. The response plan does not mention it. What should happen to the response plan?",options:["Update it with the new failure mode, its trigger, the recommended response and the responsible person","Leave it unchanged, because it was approved at project sign-off","Delete the response plan and rely on the engineer's experience","Add the cause only to the engineer's personal notes"],answer:0,hint:"Response plans are living documents updated with new information as it becomes available.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "Response Plans are living documents updated with new information as it becomes available." The control plan is also described as living.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Add the new failure mode with its trigger, response and owner (and update the FMEA) so the next responder does not start from zero."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:`Sign-off does not freeze the plan; relying on one person's memory recreates the "hero" problem.`}],final:"Living document — update it → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The response plan is a living document; sign-off does not freeze it.","Relying on individual experience is exactly what the plan is meant to replace.","Personal notes are not shared; the next responder would not see them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 562, 571"},{id:"sv-5-360",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An Xbar chart of splice loss signals at night. The night-shift technicians have never been shown the response plan, so they log the alarm and carry on. Which gap does the book point to?",options:["The chart should use 2σ limits so the night shift notices it","The response plan should be shorter","Responding to poor process results must be part of training","The night shift should stop plotting the chart"],answer:2,hint:'Book: "Response to poor process results are a must in training."',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "Response to poor process results are a must in training." The training plan also lists "part of the response plan when monitoring shows performance degrading" as a trigger for training.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A plan the responders do not know cannot be followed. Everyone who may see a signal, on every shift, must be trained in the response."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Narrower limits create false alarms; a shorter plan or no chart does not fix untrained responders."}],final:"Train people in the response → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Changing to 2σ limits adds false alarms; the real issue is that nobody knows how to respond.","The length of the plan is not the issue; the night shift has never been trained on it.","","Stopping the chart removes the signal instead of fixing the response."],bookRef:"Book pp. 565, 571"}];export{e as items};
